  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 147 PM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nModel continues to be in good agreement through the end of the work week, \nwith transitory upper ridging building across eastern Colorado through \nThursday, as southwest flow increases across western Colorado ahead of an \neastern Pacific storm system digging into the Great Basin. Model data supports\nabundant sup-tropical moisture becoming entrained within the southwest flow \naloft, with PWATS of 0.8 to over 1 inch (200-300 percent of normal) moving \nacross the region through Thursday. This, along with increasing orographic \nflow, and uvv associated with the Pacific system moving across the Great Basin, \nwill lead to increasing coverage and intensity of precipitation along and \nwest of the Continental Divide Wednesday night, with the heaviest precipitation \nexpected Thursday into Thursday night, as the main system starts to lift out\nacross the Great Basin into the Northern Rockies. \n\nLatest model data continues to support qpf of 1 to 3 inches along and west \nof the ContDvd through this period, with the greatest amounts expected across \nthe San Juan Mtns, where locally up to 4 inches of liquid precipitation will\nbe possible. The Pacific origin of the system is expected to keep snow levels \naround peak level (at and above 12,000 feet) through the duration of the heavy \nprecipitation event (at least through Thursday night), leading to concerns of \nriver and stream flooding as well as the potential for rock/mudslides across \nthe higher terrain of the ContDvd. The area of greatest concern remains across \nthe San Juan Mtns, with already saturated soils from previous precipitation \nlast week, as well the potential for heavy rain on top of snowpack already in \nplace, with SNOTEL data indicating SWE 3/4 to 1\" across the San Juans at this \ntime. With that said, we have issued a Flood Watch for the eastern San Juans \nand the Upper Rio Grande Valley (Zones 68 and 67) starting at 18Z Wednesday\nwith the potential for heavy rainfall from thunderstorms, and continuing \nthrough the day Friday, when said embedded sub-tropical moisture plume pushes\neast of the area. Those living or traveling across the southwest mountains \nshould stay weather aware through the rest of the week, and have multiple\nways to receive weather warnings. \n    \nFurther east, with the transitory ridge, warm and mainly dry conditions will\nbe in place, with chances of showers spreading east across the plains in \nlater Thursday, as the Pacific system lifts out across the Northern Rockies. \n\nWhile models continue to indicate run to run differences, however latest data \nsupports the main system lifting north and east across the Northern Rockies \non Friday, with secondary energy hanging back across the Four Corners region. \nThe northern passing system sends a front down across the plains on Saturday,\nas the secondary energy slowly lifts out across the region through the day \nMonday. This will bring much cooler temperatures and periods rain and snow \n(snow levels down to between 8000-9000 feet) through early next week.",
